---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "c4 protein powder",
      "c4 whey protein",
      "c4 reese's protein powder",
      "c4 protein powder side effects"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">C4 Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      The pre-workout brand now makes a whey with official Hershey's and Reese's flavors. Here is what is in the tub, whether it contains caffeine, which size to buy and when a rival is the better deal.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#maker">Who Makes C4 Protein</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Tub</a>
      <a href="#side-effects">Side Effects and Caffeine</a>
      <a href="#flavors">The Hershey's and Reese's Flavors</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Certification</a>
      <a href="#compare">C4 vs Ghost, Ryse and ON</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          C4 Whey Protein is a straightforward whey concentrate and isolate blend with 25 g of protein, about 130 to 140 calories and no caffeine, per the label at the time of writing. The licensed Hershey's and Reese's flavors are the reason to buy it. The 66-serving tub is fairly priced at about $1.36 to $1.60 per 25 g of protein, the 28-serving tub is not, and it is not certified for drug-tested sport.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on C4 Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            C4 Whey Protein is a competent, ordinary whey with an unusually good flavor license. Per the label at the time of writing, every flavor gives 25 g of protein per scoop, about 130 calories for chocolate and vanilla and 140 for Reese's, 0 g of added sugar and roughly 2 g of fat. The protein comes from whey concentrate and whey isolate, in that order. There is nothing exotic in the tub, and nothing missing either.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason to buy it is the flavor. C4 is one of the few protein brands with official Hershey's Milk Chocolate and Reese's Peanut Butter and Chocolate flavors, and buyer feedback suggests the chocolate in particular tastes more like a chocolate milk than a typical protein shake. If a better-tasting shake is what keeps you hitting your protein target, that is worth something real.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason to hesitate is the price of the small tub. At the prices we saw, the 28-serving tubs work out at about $1.68 to $1.96 per 25 g of protein, which is no bargain. The 66-serving tubs drop to about $1.36 to $1.59, which is competitive with mainstream wheys such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard. In other words, C4 Whey is good value only if you commit to the big tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two other things matter. First, despite the C4 name, the whey contains no caffeine and no stimulants, so the side-effect worries people bring over from C4 pre-workout do not apply. Second, the packaging carries NSF Contents Certified, which checks what is in the tub, but it is not NSF Certified for Sport, the program drug-tested athletes need. We rank C4 on its label, testing status and cost per 25 g; we have not tasted it, so taste notes are summarized buyer feedback. For the wider market, see our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good chocolate whey, fairly priced in the big tub.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy the 66-serving Hershey's Milk Chocolate tub if you want a whey that tastes like dessert. If you are drug tested or sensitive to lactose, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 is the better buy.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="maker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes C4 Protein, and What It Is Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            C4 is owned by Nutrabolt, the Texas company behind Cellucor, the original maker of C4 pre-workout, along with the XTEND amino acid brand. For years C4 meant one thing: a stimulant pre-workout, later joined by C4 Energy cans in supermarkets and gas stations. That history is why so many people searching for C4 protein powder are also searching for side effects. They assume it is a pre-workout with protein added. It is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            C4 Whey Protein arrived in the fall of 2024 as part of a licensing deal with The Hershey Company. Nutrabolt announced the collaboration in September 2024, covering energy drinks, pre-workout and protein powder in Hershey's and Reese's flavors. Industry coverage at launch described C4 Whey as the first C4 product that was neither an energy drink nor a pre-workout. The range has since expanded to the UK and Europe, where tubs carry the same Hershey's branding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The US range is small. There is one powder, C4 Whey Protein, in three flavors: Hershey's Milk Chocolate, Reese's Peanut Butter and Chocolate, and Vanilla Bean, which is sold on Amazon simply as Vanilla. Grocery chains such as H-E-B and Safeway-group stores carry 18-serving tubs of roughly 1.4 to 1.6 lb, and Amazon and online retailers carry 28-serving tubs of about 2.1 to 2.5 lb and 66-serving tubs of about 5 to 5.9 lb, depending on flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            In July 2026 Nutrabolt added C4 Sparkling Protein, a 16 oz canned drink with 20 g of clear whey protein, zero sugar and, per the launch announcement, no caffeine. It is a different product from the powder, sold first through Walmart and C4's own site and now listed on Amazon, and we do not cover it in detail here. Cellucor also sells a separate whey for professional athletes, which comes up in the testing section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway: C4 Whey is a mainstream sports-nutrition whey from a large, established company, not a boutique brand and not a stimulant product. Judge it the way you would judge any flavored whey, on label, price per gram and testing, and ignore the energy-drink branding on the front of the tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. C4 Whey Products and Alternatives, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            C4 sells one protein powder, C4 Whey Protein, in three flavors: Hershey's Milk Chocolate, Reese's Peanut Butter and Chocolate, and Vanilla Bean. Amazon carries 28- and 66-serving tubs, and grocery stores carry smaller 18-serving tubs. The three C4 cards below are the Amazon listings we would buy, followed by two clearly labelled alternatives for readers who want sport certification or a pure isolate. Prices are the ones we saw at the time of writing and change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in the Tub: The Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            We read the US nutrition panels for all three flavors as published by retailers at the time of writing. The figures below are per scoop. Scoop sizes differ because the Reese's flavor carries peanut flour and the chocolate carries cocoa, so the brand adds more powder to reach the same 25 g of protein. Labels can change, and some retailer listings show slightly different figures, so check the panel on the tub you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hershey's Milk Chocolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reese's PB and Chocolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla Bean</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scoop size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40.2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">34.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / sugar</td>
