              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein is whey protein concentrate followed by whey protein isolate in all three flavors. Ingredients are listed by weight, so there is more concentrate than isolate. That is normal for a mid-priced whey and is not a quality problem: concentrate is a complete, high-leucine protein. It does carry more lactose and fat than isolate, which explains the 2 g of fat and 80 mg of cholesterol per scoop. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate guide</a> explains when that difference matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the chocolate label is short: cocoa powder, cocoa processed with alkali, natural and artificial flavors, sunflower lecithin for mixing, salt, xanthan gum, sucralose and acesulfame potassium. The Reese's version adds partially defatted peanut flour, potassium chloride and cellulose gum, which is why it shows 490 mg of potassium against 220 mg in the chocolate. Vanilla swaps the cocoa for natural flavors and vanilla bean. The US tubs use sunflower lecithin, so soy is not listed as an allergen.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is not on the label is as useful as what is. There are no added BCAAs, creatine, enzymes, probiotics, MCT oil or fiber blends, and C4 does not publish a leucine figure. That keeps the product simple, but it also means C4 is not giving you anything extra for the money. Rivals such as Ghost add digestive enzymes including lactase, and Ryse adds MCTs and prebiotic fiber. If you just want protein and flavor, the simpler label is fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-effects" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. C4 Protein Powder Side Effects: No Caffeine, but Read This
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest misconception about C4 protein powder is that it contains caffeine. It does not. The ingredient lists we read for all three US flavors contain no caffeine, no stimulants and none of the pre-workout ingredients C4 is known for, such as beta-alanine, which causes the tingling some people feel from pre-workout. C4's energy products are a different story: Amazon listings at the time of writing show 200 mg of caffeine in a C4 Energy can and 300 mg in C4 Ultimate Energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the side effects to think about are the ordinary ones for a flavored whey. The most common is digestive discomfort, gas or bloating, usually from lactose. Because C4 lists concentrate first, it contains more lactose than an isolate. If dairy milk bothers you, try a half scoop first, mix with water or lactose-free milk, or choose an isolate. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder picks</a> list options that are much easier on sensitive stomachs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is the sweetener system. C4 uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, both approved by the FDA, and a 25 g scoop contains small amounts. Some people report bloating or a lingering sweet aftertaste from sucralose, and some simply prefer to avoid artificial sweeteners. If that is you, C4 Whey is not the right product; our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> covers stevia, monk fruit and unsweetened options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is allergies. Every flavor contains milk, and the Reese's flavor contains peanut flour, so anyone with a peanut allergy should avoid it outright. If you have a severe peanut allergy, ask C4 how the chocolate and vanilla are handled in relation to peanuts before buying, because all three flavors are made by the same brand and the allergen statement on the tub is the only reliable guide. Some people also notice dairy-related skin breakouts; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-acne/">protein powder for acne</a> guide covers that link.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the usual medical caveats apply. Protein powder is a food, but anyone with kidney disease, anyone who is pregnant or breastfeeding, and anyone on a protein-restricted diet should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding shakes. This review is not medical advice. For a broader look at what the research says about protein supplements, see <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">C4 Whey has no caffeine.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any jitters, sleep trouble or tingling you associate with C4 come from its pre-workouts and energy drinks, not the whey. The whey's likely side effects are the usual ones: lactose-related bloating, a sucralose aftertaste and allergies to milk or, in Reese's, peanuts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Hershey's and Reese's Flavors: What Buyers Say
          </h2>

          <p>
            We have not tasted C4 Whey, so this section summarizes buyer and reviewer feedback. The pattern is consistent: Hershey's Milk Chocolate gets the strongest reviews of the three. Buyers describe it as sweet, milky and close to chocolate milk, especially when mixed with milk rather than water. The most common complaint is that it is too sweet for some people, which is predictable with sucralose and acesulfame potassium together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reese's Peanut Butter and Chocolate is more divisive. Some buyers call it the best protein flavor they have tried; others say it is a decent chocolate peanut butter shake but does not really taste like a Reese's cup. The peanut flour helps, adding body and a nuttier note, but no powder fully reproduces a peanut butter cup. Our <a href="/best-peanut-butter-protein-powder/">best peanut butter protein powder</a> guide compares it with other peanut flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vanilla Bean draws fewer comments, which is usually a good sign for a vanilla. Buyers use it in smoothies, overnight oats and coffee, where the chocolate would clash. If you want a vanilla specifically, our <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">best vanilla protein powder</a> rankings include options without artificial sweeteners, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-coffee/">protein powder for coffee</a> guide covers how to mix whey into hot drinks without clumping.
          </p>

          <p>
            On mixing, buyers generally report that C4 Whey dissolves easily in a shaker, helped by the sunflower lecithin, with little foam. The xanthan gum gives it a slightly thicker body than a bare whey, which most people read as creamier. The Reese's tub, with its peanut flour and cellulose gum, is the thickest of the three. Use the label's 6 fl oz of liquid for a rich shake and 8 to 10 oz if you prefer it thinner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our advice: start with Hershey's Milk Chocolate unless you know you love peanut butter flavors. It is the flavor with the clearest buyer consensus and the cheapest large tub. If chocolate is your priority, our <a href="/best-chocolate-protein-powder/">best chocolate protein powder</a> guide ranks the strongest chocolate wheys on taste feedback, label and price.
          </p>
