        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy C4 Whey, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy C4 Whey if flavor is what decides whether you actually drink your protein. The Hershey's Milk Chocolate tub in the 66-serving size gives you a well-liked chocolate whey at about $1.36 per 25 g, a fair price for a licensed flavor from a major brand. It suits lifters, busy people topping up protein and anyone who finds standard wheys boring. If you are unsure how much you need, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein calculator guide</a> helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport. NSF Contents Certified is not a banned-substance certification, and we could not find C4 Whey in the Certified for Sport or Informed Choice databases. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 is the safer choice, and either costs about the same or a little more per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if lactose bothers you. A concentrate-first blend is not the best choice for a sensitive stomach, and C4 adds no lactase or other enzymes. A pure isolate such as ISO100, or a whey with added lactase such as Ghost, is a better starting point. Skip it, too, if you avoid sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and skip the Reese's flavor completely if you or anyone sharing your shaker has a peanut allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not buy C4 Whey expecting an energy boost. It has no caffeine, so it will not replace your pre-workout or morning coffee, and it is not meant to. And do not buy the small 28-serving tub as your regular purchase: use it to choose a flavor, then move to the 66-serving tub, where C4 becomes genuinely good value.
          </p>
        </div>

