              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                C4 Whey shares a name with C4 pre-workout and energy drinks, but the protein contains no caffeine or stimulants, per the ingredient lists we read. Do not skip it in the evening for fear of losing sleep, and do not expect it to replace your pre-workout. Check the label if you pick up a different C4 product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 28-serving tub every time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At the prices we saw, the 28-serving chocolate tub costs about $1.96 per 25 g, against about $1.36 for the 66-serving tub. That is roughly $40 more for the same 66 shakes. Use a small tub to pick a flavor, then switch to the large one for regular use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF Contents Certified as sport-safe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NSF Contents Certified verifies label claims and screens for contaminants. It does not screen for substances banned in sport the way NSF Certified for Sport does. Drug-tested athletes should choose a whey listed in the Certified for Sport or Informed Choice databases for the exact flavor and lot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the peanut flour in Reese's</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Reese's flavor contains partially defatted peanut flour, not just peanut flavoring, so it is unsafe for anyone with a peanut allergy. Store it apart from other tubs and wash shakers between uses if anyone in the house is allergic. Choose Hershey's Milk Chocolate or Vanilla instead if in doubt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming the protein for lactose trouble</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If C4 Whey leaves you bloated, the concentrate-first blend is a likely reason, since concentrate carries more lactose than isolate. Try half a scoop in water first. If that still bothers you, switch to a pure isolate or a whey with added lactase rather than giving up on whey entirely.
